  "statement": "A government-run certification regime for AI toys collapses on day one because public-sector agencies cannot hire the engineers needed to audit black-box models. Seven established AI companies, including Google and Meta, now account for more than 35 percent of the entire S&P 500 market capitalization according to Transparency in AI is on the decline. These corporate giants pay starting engineering salaries exceeding four hundred thousand dollars. The Federal Trade Commission and Consumer Product Safety Commission offer federal pay scales that top out at roughly one hundred sixty thousand dollars. Federal regulators cannot police code they cannot read, staffed by personnel they cannot retain.\n\nProponents of pre-market testing ignore how administrative capture operates in practice when technical expertise concentrates entirely within the regulated industry. A revolving door ensures that the nominal public watchdogs spend their tenures anticipating lucrative post-government employment at the exact firms whose products they currently certify. Compliance audits become bureaucratic theater where self-reported testing data substitutes for independent verification. Independent technical auditors cannot inspect proprietary weight matrices or opaque training pipelines because model parameters remain locked behind proprietary APIs. The resulting regulatory moat protects dominant market players by weaponizing compliance costs against smaller competitors.\n\nPretending that a fee-funded certification board can outpace adaptive software architecture is an exercise in willful blindness. Runtime parameter updates render static pre-market tests obsolete the moment a connected toy connects to Wi-Fi and updates its local weights. A compliance certificate issued on Tuesday covers an entirely different neural network by Thursday afternoon. The statutory liability assignment proposed in this policy merely shifts the cost of software defects onto consumers while shielding corporate executives from criminal negligence. No statutory fee structure generates enough revenue to buy the talent or compute required to reverse-engineer a proprietary transformer model before it hits store shelves.\n\nWhile the debate fixates on theoretical safety proofs, real children receive conversational doll outputs trained on scraped web data filtered by low-wage human contractors. A twenty-three-year-old worker in Nairobi labels toxic dialogue streams for two dollars an hour so corporate guardrails pass surface-level audits. That labor reality exposes the moral bankruptcy of treating safety as a paperwork exercise administered by a starved federal agency. Let the proponents explain which junior compliance officer at the Consumer Product Safety Commission possesses the cluster compute required to audit a multi-billion parameter model before the holiday shopping season.",
